## 4. AI Coding Assistants

These are the main tools for using AI while actually writing code.

| Tool | Free Tier | Best For | Link |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Claude Code** | Paid, or free with local / alternative model setup | Terminal-based agentic coding with repo context | https://claude.com/product/claude-code |
| **Continue** | Fully free | VS Code / JetBrains with local or cloud models | https://continue.dev |
| **Cursor** | Free tier available | IDE-style AI coding and quick prototyping | https://cursor.com |
| **Aider** | Fully free | Terminal + Git-based coding workflows | https://aider.chat |
| **Windsurf** | Limited free tier | Agentic IDE for larger codebases | https://windsurf.com |
| **Jules** | Limited free tier | Autonomous coding agent with GitHub integration | https://jules.google.com |
| **GitHub Copilot** | Free tier available | In-editor suggestions inside VS Code and other IDEs | https://github.com/features/copilot |

### Which one should you use?

#### Best for most builders
- **Claude Code** if you want structured build workflows, strong reasoning, and multi-step implementation
- **Cursor** if you want fast in-IDE assistance with low friction
- **Continue** if you want a fully open setup with local models

#### Best for terminal-first workflows
- **Claude Code**
- **Aider**

#### Best for quick hackathon-style iteration
- **Cursor**
- **Claude Code**
- **Google AI Studio** for long-context planning/debugging

For StellarX Philippines, if your goal is to go from idea to demo quickly, the most practical default combo is:
- **Claude Code or Cursor**
- **Stella**
- **Free_AI_Setup.md** if you need zero-cost model access

